    "found 0 pin-name label(s) on any of this deck's label layers (5/1, 8/25, 10/25, 30/25, 50/25, 67/25, 126/25, 134/25) anywhere in 'sg13_lv_pmos' -- no net can be named, so 0 top-level pins will be promoted below and `klt lvs` will have no net/device anchor to seed a match against a reference netlist. Compare the layers `klt layers` reports for this GDS against the list above; for a `klt place-and-route` layout in particular, check that request.io.layer_h/layer_v chose a layer this --deck actually scans for pin labels (issue #1385)",
    "0 top-level pins are promoted on 'sg13_lv_pmos' after extraction -- `klt lvs` has no net/device anchor to seed correspondence against a reference netlist and will report a full mismatch regardless of device-count agreement. If this design genuinely has zero top-level pins by intent, this warning can be ignored; otherwise see the pin-name-label warning above (if present) or check that --top-cell-pins/--pins/--def-pins did not demote every promoted pin (issue #1385)",
    "16 PMOS devices tie their body to an anonymous net with no DC bias path -- no drawn (or derivable) well tie connects this PMOS body to a real supply rail on this layout, so it is left floating rather than tied to a real supply rail; resimulating this netlist directly will not reproduce the schematic-level PMOS body bias -- see unbiased_pmos_body_nets[] for the full list. See docs/cli/extract.md's 'Parasitic (RC) extraction' section.",
    "14 nets connect to exactly one device terminal -- a MOS gate -- and are not a declared pin: these gates have no DC path from anywhere else in the netlist, so they are almost certainly unconnected inputs rather than legitimate floating nodes; resimulating this netlist directly will hit a singular matrix on these nets -- see single_terminal_nets[] for the full list.",
